**Merge sort.** Divide-and-conquer: split the array into halves, sort each recursively, then **merge** the two sorted halves by repeatedly taking the smaller front element. Time **O(n log n)** in all cases; extra space O(n); **stable**. Good for linked lists and external (disk) sorting, with guaranteed performance.

**Quick sort.** Choose a **pivot**, partition the array so that elements smaller than the pivot go left and larger go right (the pivot lands at its final position), then recursively sort both sides. Average **O(n log n)**; worst case **O(n^2)** when partitions are very unbalanced (for example, always picking the first element on an already sorted array). Fix with a random pivot or median-of-three. In place (O(log n) stack on average), usually the fastest in practice because of cache-friendly partitioning, but **not stable**.

| | Best | Average | Worst | Space | Stable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Merge sort | n log n | n log n | n log n | O(n) | yes |
| Quick sort | n log n | n log n | n^2 | O(log n) | no |
| Bubble sort | n (with early exit) | n^2 | n^2 | O(1) | yes |

**Bubble sort (on paper).** Repeatedly compare adjacent elements and swap them if out of order; after each pass the largest remaining element "bubbles" to the end. Stop early if a pass makes no swaps.

```c
void bubble_sort(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int swapped = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
                int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;       /* already sorted */
    }
}
```

On [5, 1, 4, 2]: pass 1 gives [1, 4, 2, 5]; pass 2 gives [1, 2, 4, 5]; pass 3 makes no swaps, so it stops.

**When to choose:** merge sort for stability or guaranteed O(n log n); quick sort for in-memory arrays and speed; bubble sort only for teaching or tiny, nearly sorted inputs.
